From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gustavo Morinaga <me@gustavomorinaga.dev>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 18:45:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Vulkan: a 32-bit word form of the MLAA shader for AMD's
 Windows driver, and a compile marker instead of the driver check

Patch 2086 left the MLAA pass out on AMD's Windows driver: its shader
compiler ended the process on rsx_vk_mlaa.comp every time (#129; RX 6600,
26.10.1, amdvlk64.dll+0x771aee). What sets that shader apart from the
glow and motion blur, which build there, is the SPU float emulation on
64-bit integers (GL_ARB_gpu_shader_int64).

rsx_vk_mlaa32.comp is the same shader with those 64-bit values kept as
uvec2 (low and high word: umulExtended, uaddCarry, usubBorrow) and no
Int64 capability. The output is the same, byte for byte: on an RTX 3070 Ti
both forms match each other and the C reference on 7 images and on
tools/mlaa_harness's regression at 1280x720, 2560x1440, 3440x1440,
1707x960 and 1281x721. It is 5-30 % slower there, so vk_mlaa_spv gives
it only to AMD's Windows driver (driver id 1) and to devices without
shaderInt64 (which before got no MLAA at all); the rest keep the 64-bit
form. PS3RECOMP_MLAA_SHADER=32 or 64 picks one. On the RX 6600 that
closed the game, the 32-bit form built in 13.9 s on a cold cache and ran
through level 1.

The driver check goes. In its place, a marker file in the shader cache
folder (mlaa-compile.pending) names the driver (vendor, device, driver
version) and the shader (FNV-1a of its SPIR-V) while the compile runs,
and is removed when it returns. Found at the next start for the same
driver and shader, the compile never returned, and the MLAA is not
built again there (the log says so; the glow and motion blur run, the
PC screen greys Anti-aliasing). A new driver or shader tries again.
PS3RECOMP_MLAA_BUILD=1 builds it whatever the marker says, 0 never.
